      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm stuck on my lit review. if i have a source with like 5 authors, how do i do the in-text citation for APA 7th vs chicago? i keep getting them mixed up.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're absolutely right to be confused, but don't worry, because the rules for citations are actually quite straightforward for a team of five authors, though they do require a bit of mental gymnastics to keep the right style in your head.\n\nFor **APA 7th**, you don't need to list all five names every time you mention the source. Instead, you list the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" (meaning \"and others\") for any citation with two or more authors. So, if you have a source by Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, and Green, your in-text citation would simply be (Smith et al.). This is much easier to type than writing out five names, but it's important to remember that the first author's name is the key identifier here.\n\nIn contrast, **Chicago Style** (specifically the Notes-Bibliography system, which is the most common for humanities) is a bit more verbose. You generally need to list all the authors' last names in the order they appear on the page, separated by commas, unless you are using the author-date parenthetical system, in which case you might list the first author followed by \"et al.\" depending on how",
