      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for chicago, if i'm doing the footnotes/notes version, do i still use et al. for 5 authors or do i have to list them all in the footnote? also does it change for the bibliography at the end?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are very welcome! It is a common struggle to keep the different systems straight, but you are on the right track.\n\nFor **Chicago Notes-Bibliography**, the rules are actually quite similar to APA regarding the number of authors, but the presentation differs slightly. If you have five authors, you generally **do not** list all five names in the footnote or parenthetical citation unless the work is very short (like a newspaper article) or you are specifically instructed to do so for clarity. Instead, you use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" (meaning \"and others\") just like in APA. So, if your source is by Smith, Jones, Doe, Brown, and Green, your footnote would look like: *Smith et al., 2023*.\n\nHowever, the **bibliography** (or Works Cited) at the end of the document is where the real work happens. In the bibliography, you **must** list all five authors' full names in the order they appear on the source, separated by commas. You cannot use \"et al.\" in the bibliography entry itself; the full list is required there to ensure anyone reading your paper can identify the specific work.",
